    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 5\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[29, 22, 21], [11, 30, 12], [11, 21, 19], [12, 30, 16], [23, 26, 29]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 1, 0], [0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 2], [0, 2, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Production Planning, Shop Floor Supervisors, and Maintenance\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated Scheduling of Current Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\n\nContext\nWe operate a multi-machine job shop with specialized workstations. The current workload consists of multiple customer orders that must pass through a defined sequence of operations. Each step in a job\u2019s route requires a specific machine and a known processing duration. Given limited, shared machine capacity and the fixed routing for each job, we need to coordinate start and finish times to avoid bottlenecks, idle time, and unnecessary work-in-process.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe core challenge is to place every operation on the appropriate machine timeline so that:\n- No two operations compete for the same machine at the same time.\n- Each job\u2019s operations occur in the required order.\n- Machines and teams are utilized efficiently to compress overall completion.\n\nOperating Rules and Assumptions\n- Fixed routing by job: Each job follows a predefined sequence of operations that must be performed in order.\n- Machine-specific work: Each operation is assigned to a particular machine and must be performed on that machine.\n- Known processing durations: Each operation has a defined run time.\n- Exclusive machine use: A machine processes only one operation at a time; overlapping or parallel processing on the same machine is not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n- Feasibility: All scheduled start times must respect the above rules; operations may queue but cannot jump ahead of required predecessors.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a feasible, conflict-free production schedule that assigns start and finish times to every operation so that all jobs are completed in the shortest possible overall time (makespan), while honoring machine availability, operation sequences, and non-interruption requirements.\n\nNext Steps\n- Confirm operation sequences, machine assignments, and processing durations for each job as outlined in the annex.\n- Generate a baseline schedule that sequences operations on each machine without conflicts and respects job order.\n- Review the baseline for hotspots (long queues, underutilized machines) and iterate to compress the makespan.\n- Communicate the finalized start windows and handoff expectations to supervisors for execution and daily control.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 5\n- Number of machines: 3\n- Machine assignments by job and operation (required workstation per step): Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1]\n- Processing times by job and operation (run time per step): Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 29, Op2: 22, Op3: 21]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 11, Op2: 30, Op3: 12]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 11, Op2: 21, Op3: 19]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 12, Op2: 30, Op3: 16]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: 23, Op2: 26, Op3: 29]",
